<h1>Editing run configuration</h1>
<p>This section walks through editing a single, simple config field - <code>Cycles</code>. The same principles
  apply to every parameter we'll encounter later in this tutorial, including more complex ones like matrices.</p>

<h2 id="editing-config-parameter">Editing config parameter</h2>

<h3 id="selecting-test-vs-test-run">Selecting Test vs Test Run</h3>
<p>First of all, let's look into the execution tree and select the test node itself, not the test run instance.
  The difference is that the test node (<strong>Hello World</strong>) is the test's definition, shared by every
  run, while the test run instance (<strong>Test Run: Hello World</strong>) is one specific execution of it, with
  its own status and results.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/test-selection.png", "Selecting the Hello World test node, as opposed to its test run instance, in the Orcraft controller's execution tree", "large") }}

<h3 id="changing-a-parameter">Changing a parameter</h3>
<p>Let's focus on the configuration tab, next to the log. Currently, you can see only one config parameter,
  <code>Cycles</code>. If you remember, we didn't define it in the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/hello-world-test-structure/#config-class">test's code</a> - that's because
  it's a built-in parameter that will always be shown for any test.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/config.png", "Configuration tab of the Hello World test in the Orcraft controller, showing the built-in Cycles field", "large") }}
<p>Let's change the number of cycles to 2:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/config-2-cycles.png", "Cycles field of the Hello World test set to 2 in the Orcraft controller", "large") }}
<p>As you can see, the <code class="text-purple">Cycles</code> field is now purple - meaning it changed from its
  default - and an <strong class="text-orange">orange dot</strong> appears next to it, meaning the configuration
  is now different from the plan. Notice that <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> and
  <strong class="text-muted">Reset To Defaults</strong> both became available. We won't press either of those for
  now.</p>
<p>Also notice that <strong>Total Cases</strong> is now 2 as well. We'll talk about matrices and cases later on,
  since they also affect this number - for now, just get familiar with the formula, no need to remember it yet;
  see the recap in the <a class="text-accent" href="#total-number-of-cases-formula-recap">chapter's summary</a>
  below.</p>

<h3 id="running-unplanned">Running unplanned</h3>
<p>Let's click <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/plan-mismatch.png", "Execution tree showing a second Hello World test run instance with a Plan Mismatch badge", "large") }}
<p>As you can see, another test run instance appeared, with a <strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong>
  badge - indicating that something changed since the plan was last updated (maybe by mistake, or just to try
  something out), so this run isn't included in the statistics. The previous run is dimmed because it was
  superseded by this new run on the same endpoint configuration (which is just <code>localhost</code> here).</p>

<h3 id="updating-the-plan">Updating the plan</h3>
<p>Now let's click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, and click <strong>Yes</strong> on the
  confirmation dialog:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/update-plan.png", "Execution tree and configuration panel of the Hello World test after updating the plan", "large") }}
<p>Now we see <code class="text-accent">Cycles</code> is blue, meaning this is the new plan, different from the
  default. Pay attention that the <strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong> badge went to the previous
  run, since the last run now matches the plan (2 cycles) and previous one doesn't (1 cycle). Also notice that the
  statistics columns now show 2 in each column - the planned run is now counted.</p>
<h3 id="note-on-statistics">Note on statistics</h3>
<p>It's worth emphasizing that the statistics don't simply sum up every test run. Instead, they select which runs
  to count based on criteria such as: whether the run is planned or unplanned, whether it was superseded by a
  later run, and whether the endpoints configuration matches (we'll cover endpoints configuration later in this
  tutorial).</p>

<h3 id="resetting-to-defaults">Resetting to defaults</h3>
<p>Now let's click <strong class="text-muted">Reset To Defaults</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/reset-to-defaults.png", "Configuration panel of the Hello World test after resetting Cycles to its default value", "large") }}
<p>Now you can see that <code>Cycles</code> went back from <strong class="text-accent">blue</strong> to white,
  meaning it's back to its default value - and the <strong class="text-orange">orange dot</strong> appears again,
  because we changed the plan from the last update (from 2 cycles back to 1).</p>
<p>Finally, let's click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> once more. This brings us back to the
  default, planned state we started with:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/update-plan-after-reset-to-defaults.png", "Execution tree and configuration panel of the Hello World test after updating the plan back to its default", "large") }}

<h2 id="rerunning-test-runs">Rerunning test runs</h2>

<h3 id="rerunning-a-planned-test">Rerunning a planned test</h3>
<p>Let's click on the first planned test run instance we ran. Its configuration tab now shows the exact
  configuration it was run with, and we can rerun it with that very same configuration:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/rerun-planned.png", "Configuration panel of a planned Hello World test run instance, ready to be rerun with its original configuration", "large") }}
<p>Let's click <strong class="text-purple">Rerun</strong> and see another planned instance appear, while the two
  previous ones are now superseded:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/rerun-planned-finished.png", "Execution tree showing a new planned Hello World test run instance after rerunning, with the two previous runs superseded", "large") }}

<h3 id="rerunning-an-unplanned-test">Rerunning an unplanned test</h3>
<p>Let's click on the <strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong> test run instance instead. Its
  configuration tab shows <code class="text-accent">Cycles</code> set to 2, different from the current plan (1) -
  this run was unplanned, but its configuration is still preserved and can be rerun just the same:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/rerun-unplanned.png", "Configuration panel of an unplanned Hello World test run instance, showing its Cycles value different from the current plan", "large") }}
<p>Also notice that <strong class="text-muted">Reset To Defaults</strong> is enabled now, since 2 isn't the
  default value (1 is).</p>

<h3 id="resetting-to-run-settings">Resetting to Run Settings</h3>
<p>Now let's say we change this previous run's config to 3 cycles:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/editing-run-configuration/rerun-changed-test-run-config.png", "Configuration panel of a Hello World test run instance after changing its Cycles field to 3", "large") }}
<p>You can see that it now appears as different from both the default and the plan, and that
  <strong class="text-muted">Reset To Run Settings</strong> is enabled too. Clicking it takes us back to this run's
  original configuration.</p>
<p>It's worth emphasizing that each test run instance carries its own configuration, independent of the test's
  current plan. Whether a given run shows up as planned or unplanned depends solely on whether its own
  configuration happens to match the plan at the time - not on which one you're looking at.</p>

<h2 id="chapters-summary">Chapter's summary</h2>
<h3 id="key-takeaways">Key takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The test node holds the current plan; each test run instance is a separate execution with its own,
    independent configuration.</li>
  <li>A field's color shows how its value compares to the default and to the plan (see the legend below).</li>
  <li><strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> makes the current configuration the new plan.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-muted">Reset To Defaults</strong> and <strong class="text-muted">Reset To Run
    Settings</strong> revert a field to its default or to the run's original value, respectively.</li>
  <li><strong class="text-purple">Rerun</strong> runs a test run instance again with its own preserved
    configuration, whether planned or not.</li>
  <li>Statistics only count runs that meet certain criteria - planned vs. unplanned, superseded or not, and
    matching endpoints configuration.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="field-and-badge-legend">Field and badge legend</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Indicator</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>White field</td>
      <td>Value matches its default</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="text-purple">Purple field</code></td>
      <td>Value changed from its default</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="text-accent">Blue field</code></td>
      <td>Value matches the plan, which differs from the default</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong class="text-orange">Orange dot</strong></td>
      <td>Configuration differs from the plan</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong> badge</td>
      <td>This run's configuration didn't match the plan when it ran, so it's excluded from statistics</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> / <strong class="text-purple">Rerun</strong></td>
      <td>Starts a new run, or reruns an existing run instance, with the shown configuration</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong></td>
      <td>Makes the current configuration the new plan</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong class="text-muted">Reset To Defaults</strong></td>
      <td>Reverts fields to their default values</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong class="text-muted">Reset To Run Settings</strong></td>
      <td>Reverts a test run instance's fields to the values it was originally run with</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

